1
00:00:00,842 --> 00:00:02,803
Sync and corrections by n17t01
www.addic7ed.com

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:04,351
(Crows cawing)

3
00:00:05,274 --> 00:00:06,909
Have you guys seen my iPad?

4
00:00:07,315 --> 00:00:08,557
<i>(Frankie)</i>
<i>I know what you're thinking.</i>

5
00:00:08,672 --> 00:00:11,023
<i>80% of our appliances</i>
<i>have duct tape on 'em.</i>

6
00:00:11,128 --> 00:00:13,088
<i>How the hell did Brick</i>
<i>get an iPad?</i>

7
00:00:13,390 --> 00:00:15,112
<i>Well, when a kid</i>
<i>really wants something,</i>

8
00:00:15,216 --> 00:00:18,140
<i>they chip away at you like</i>
<i>a lumberjack on a redwood.</i>

9
00:00:18,276 --> 00:00:20,470
<i>I guess it started</i>
<i>about a month ago.</i>

10
00:00:20,812 --> 00:00:23,061
(Laughs)

11
00:00:23,733 --> 00:00:26,194
- What's so funny?
- Hey, Mike, get in here.

12
00:00:26,292 --> 00:00:28,485
Go ahead. Ask your dad
what you just asked me.

13
00:00:28,770 --> 00:00:30,449
Can you guys buy me an iPad?

14
00:00:30,587 --> 00:00:32,402
(Laughs)

15
00:00:33,288 --> 00:00:35,169
- Thanks, buddy. I needed that.
- Yeah.

16
00:00:35,724 --> 00:00:37,352
- See you guys tonight.
- Okay.

17
00:00:37,857 --> 00:00:39,843
(Laughs) "Can you guys
buy me an iPad?"

18
00:00:40,078 --> 00:00:42,696
- Yeah. (Laughs)
- So the iPad? Yes or no?

19
00:00:42,806 --> 00:00:44,935
(Door opens and closes) Brick, the
only way you're getting an iPad

20
00:00:45,057 --> 00:00:47,105
is if you're in
Steve Jobs' will.

21
00:00:47,325 --> 00:00:49,337
(Whistling)

22
00:00:50,462 --> 00:00:51,895
(Bottles rattle)

23
00:00:53,187 --> 00:00:54,169
What?
(Refrigerator door closes)

24
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:55,785
Today is baby day
for spirit week,

25
00:00:55,892 --> 00:00:57,648
so me, Sean, and Darrin
are rockin' the diapers.

26
00:00:57,757 --> 00:00:59,223
Baby seniors rule!
(Rattles)

27
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:01,874
Hey, uh, if you get
a chance today,

28
00:01:02,018 --> 00:01:03,569
you wanna stop by
the College Center

29
00:01:03,687 --> 00:01:05,972
and find out why we haven't
heard from East Indiana yet?

30
00:01:06,082 --> 00:01:08,361
Mom, a lot of people haven't
heard anything yet, all right?

31
00:01:08,476 --> 00:01:09,710
You don't have to worry
about this.

32
00:01:09,837 --> 00:01:11,117
I got it under control.

33
00:01:11,246 --> 00:01:13,528
I'm practically an adult, so...

34
00:01:19,315 --> 00:01:21,839
Ah! Good thing you're wearing
those diapers, dude,

35
00:01:21,956 --> 00:01:25,059
'cause guess who just got
accepted to Notre Dame.

36
00:01:25,157 --> 00:01:26,737
Wow. Maybe you'll date
a leprechaun.

37
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:27,909
It's not just him.

38
00:01:28,043 --> 00:01:30,426
You are looking at two pillars
of higher education.

39
00:01:30,610 --> 00:01:31,821
I just got my fat envelope

40
00:01:31,949 --> 00:01:34,391
from Midwest Institute
of Air Conditioning Repair.

41
00:01:34,595 --> 00:01:35,844
First choice!

42
00:01:36,259 --> 00:01:37,624
All right, so what's the story
with you, man?

43
00:01:37,728 --> 00:01:39,316
You get the good news
from East Indy yet?

44
00:01:39,437 --> 00:01:40,726
Uh, nah. Nothing official,

45
00:01:40,837 --> 00:01:43,080
but the recruiter pretty much
told me it's a lock,

46
00:01:43,218 --> 00:01:46,805
so... (Chuckles)
I am not sweating it.

47
00:01:46,913 --> 00:01:48,232
Yeah. No worries.

48
00:01:48,357 --> 00:01:49,587
If it doesn't happen,
I could probably get you in

49
00:01:49,702 --> 00:01:51,268
at MIACR.

50
00:01:51,587 --> 00:01:52,993
I'm a legacy.

51
00:01:53,567 --> 00:01:55,213
All right, men, uh,
enough college talk.

52
00:01:55,334 --> 00:01:57,298
Let's talk about something
really important.

53
00:01:57,493 --> 00:02:00,016
- Our senior prank.
- Yeah.

54
00:02:00,142 --> 00:02:01,887
I'm not sure I'm really
feeling a prank this year.

55
00:02:02,013 --> 00:02:04,499
We're seniors.
When else are we gonna do it?

56
00:02:04,615 --> 00:02:05,843
I just don't feel like it,
'kay?

57
00:02:05,955 --> 00:02:08,565
- Don't get your diapers in a twist.
- They're not diapers.

58
00:02:08,661 --> 00:02:10,061
They're adult comfort shorts.

59
00:02:10,217 --> 00:02:12,361
My grandpa wears 'em
when he plays golf.

60
00:02:12,785 --> 00:02:13,954
(School bell rings)

61
00:02:15,161 --> 00:02:17,546
<i>You know how Sue was usually</i>
<i>invisible to teachers?</i>

62
00:02:17,687 --> 00:02:19,913
<i>Well, this year,</i>
<i>she finally got noticed by one.</i>

63
00:02:20,021 --> 00:02:21,855
The bell has rung, people.

64
00:02:22,222 --> 00:02:24,933
The school may find it amusing
to have you all dressed up

65
00:02:25,045 --> 00:02:26,235
and run around
like little babies,

66
00:02:26,357 --> 00:02:29,537
but in my class, you will be
treated like the young adults

67
00:02:29,661 --> 00:02:32,434
you claim to be.
Miss Heck...

68
00:02:33,217 --> 00:02:35,115
next time you write
an essay for my class,

69
00:02:35,241 --> 00:02:38,421
let's try using one color ink
instead of 20.

70
00:02:39,016 --> 00:02:39,801
(Pens clatter)

71
00:02:39,930 --> 00:02:40,994
Oh, and I hate to rain
on your parade,

72
00:02:41,109 --> 00:02:42,559
but perhaps it's time
to stop dotting your I's

73
00:02:42,708 --> 00:02:44,672
with little hearts.
(Paper rustles)

74
00:02:44,803 --> 00:02:46,919
(Students chuckle and murmur)

75
00:02:48,736 --> 00:02:50,276
Silence!

76
00:02:50,436 --> 00:02:51,921
(Students gasp)
Do any of you have the desire

77
00:02:52,043 --> 00:02:53,471
to think outside yourselves

78
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:55,693
and to contribute to the world
you live in?

79
00:02:56,175 --> 00:02:58,288
Somebody say, "yes, I do."

80
00:02:58,415 --> 00:02:59,532
Yes, I do.

81
00:02:59,651 --> 00:03:02,094
No, you don't.
You're all pleasure seekers.

82
00:03:02,188 --> 00:03:05,214
It's me, me, me 24/7.

83
00:03:05,326 --> 00:03:07,539
You get upset if the battery
in your phone dies.

84
00:03:07,673 --> 00:03:10,981
But did you know that
Alessandro Volta

85
00:03:11,116 --> 00:03:12,992
invented the battery in 1800?

86
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:14,105
Well, of course you don't.

87
00:03:14,211 --> 00:03:16,099
You think it was invented
by a little pink bunny

88
00:03:16,276 --> 00:03:20,092
who goes around banging away
on a big bass drum.

89
00:03:20,816 --> 00:03:24,895
Now for your next assignment,
you will propose an hypothesis.

90
00:03:25,024 --> 00:03:27,475
You will design and produce
an experiment

91
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:29,421
to test said hypothesis
and determine

92
00:03:29,543 --> 00:03:31,209
whether to accept or reject it...
(Pen clicks)

93
00:03:31,316 --> 00:03:35,795
and I want this question
to come from your soul.

94
00:03:35,918 --> 00:03:37,927
Where is your passion?

95
00:03:38,077 --> 00:03:39,759
What do you care about?

96
00:03:39,928 --> 00:03:41,934
What are you
really interested in?

97
00:03:42,892 --> 00:03:44,341
The answer is not gonna be
on your computer

98
00:03:44,462 --> 00:03:45,297
or your television.

99
00:03:45,418 --> 00:03:47,924
He's not gonna pop up for you
in an app.

100
00:03:48,284 --> 00:03:51,288
This project is 40%
of your grade.

101
00:03:51,562 --> 00:03:56,305
So as you Americans say,
you had better get crackin'.

102
00:04:07,109 --> 00:04:11,189
_

103
00:04:11,496 --> 00:04:13,367
(Hair dryer whirring)

104
00:04:13,496 --> 00:04:15,468
Dad, can you please
get me an iPad?

105
00:04:16,438 --> 00:04:18,739
I'm heating up my coffee
with a hair dryer.

106
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:20,961
What do you think?
(Hair dryer turns off)

107
00:04:21,455 --> 00:04:22,194
(Electricity crackles)

108
00:04:22,301 --> 00:04:24,627
I don't think you realize what
getting me an iPad would mean.

109
00:04:24,753 --> 00:04:26,028
For example,
when we go on trips,

110
00:04:26,144 --> 00:04:27,755
you wouldn't have to
lug around all my books.

111
00:04:27,890 --> 00:04:29,096
Remember that time
you threw out your back

112
00:04:29,218 --> 00:04:30,698
while picking up my book bag?

113
00:04:31,018 --> 00:04:32,017
(Door opens)
The answer's still no,

114
00:04:32,111 --> 00:04:33,999
but thanks for reminding me I'm old.
(Door closes)

115
00:04:34,371 --> 00:04:35,211
(Sighs)

116
00:04:35,328 --> 00:04:36,851
Well?
Anything in the mail?

117
00:04:36,987 --> 00:04:38,016
Just bills.

118
00:04:38,135 --> 00:04:39,430
You know,
it wouldn't just be for me.

119
00:04:39,536 --> 00:04:41,056
The whole family could use it.
(Sighs)

120
00:04:41,197 --> 00:04:43,053
Are we still talking
about that iPad?

121
00:04:43,162 --> 00:04:44,886
You know, it was funny
the first 70 times.

122
00:04:45,007 --> 00:04:46,827
Now it's starting to get old.

123
00:04:47,624 --> 00:04:49,172
We should have heard by now.

124
00:04:49,290 --> 00:04:51,195
The guy made it sound
like it was locked up.

125
00:04:51,583 --> 00:04:53,831
I gotta tell you, Frankie, I'm
getting a little nervous here.

126
00:04:53,938 --> 00:04:55,302
You know what?
There's a little hope.

127
00:04:55,416 --> 00:04:57,162
(Refrigerator closes) One of the
girls in my dental materials class

128
00:04:57,283 --> 00:04:58,815
said that her sister
applied to East Indy

129
00:04:58,955 --> 00:05:00,239
about the same time as Axl,

130
00:05:00,361 --> 00:05:01,975
and she still
hasn't heard yet either.

131
00:05:02,202 --> 00:05:05,182
- Hmm. - If I get lost on my way
to school and need directions...

132
00:05:05,293 --> 00:05:06,226
(Sighs)
there's an app for that.

133
00:05:06,339 --> 00:05:08,041
Why are we still talking
about this? Go away.

134
00:05:08,163 --> 00:05:09,505
But, dad, you're not letting me
prove my--

135
00:05:09,606 --> 00:05:11,352
Go.
(Hair dryer resumes whirring)

136
00:05:11,948 --> 00:05:13,187
(Hair dryer turns off)

137
00:05:14,307 --> 00:05:15,327
(Sighs)

138
00:05:16,645 --> 00:05:17,691
Brad.

139
00:05:18,303 --> 00:05:20,156
How would you like to be
the first subject

140
00:05:20,289 --> 00:05:21,593
in my science experiment?

141
00:05:21,730 --> 00:05:23,225
Are you kidding?
I'd be honored.

142
00:05:23,401 --> 00:05:25,877
But I should warn you, I can't
tolerate needles or tickling.

143
00:05:26,007 --> 00:05:27,557
We should be good. Okay.

144
00:05:27,702 --> 00:05:30,330
I'll smile at you, and then
if you smile back at me,

145
00:05:30,489 --> 00:05:35,046
that proves my hypothesis
that smiling is contagious.

146
00:05:35,234 --> 00:05:36,570
Love it.

147
00:05:36,716 --> 00:05:39,105
You will be subject X.

148
00:05:39,297 --> 00:05:41,406
The first entry in my log.

149
00:05:41,524 --> 00:05:42,252
Ready?

150
00:05:42,335 --> 00:05:43,396
Ready.

151
00:05:46,004 --> 00:05:47,069
Brad?

152
00:05:47,184 --> 00:05:48,118
Yeah?

153
00:05:48,229 --> 00:05:49,655
I think you
have to stop smiling.

154
00:05:49,769 --> 00:05:51,338
Otherwise, when I smile at you,

155
00:05:51,493 --> 00:05:54,489
- we won't know if you smile back.
- Oh, right. Of course.

156
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:57,031
Okay, I'm ready. Go.

157
00:05:58,578 --> 00:06:00,771
Oh! Damn it, Bradley.
Why can't you do this?

158
00:06:00,890 --> 00:06:02,647
You went to drama camp,
for God sake.

159
00:06:02,773 --> 00:06:05,137
It's not your fault, Brad.
The reason this isn't working

160
00:06:05,246 --> 00:06:07,242
is because I told you
the experiment.

161
00:06:07,414 --> 00:06:09,348
I was just so excited,
I had to share.

162
00:06:09,493 --> 00:06:12,193
But I tainted the results.
You know what?

163
00:06:12,342 --> 00:06:14,907
I need to test my hypothesis
on strangers.

164
00:06:15,198 --> 00:06:16,270
Good luck.

165
00:06:18,125 --> 00:06:19,868
(Indistinct conversations)

166
00:06:23,609 --> 00:06:26,226
<i>So while Sue was trying to</i>
<i>prove smiling is contagious,</i>

167
00:06:26,362 --> 00:06:29,235
<i>Mike and I were proving</i>
<i>being nervous definitely was.</i>

168
00:06:29,384 --> 00:06:31,856
- Anything yet?
- Still waiting. I don't get it.

169
00:06:31,973 --> 00:06:33,725
We should have heard
something by now.

170
00:06:33,854 --> 00:06:35,067
You know that lady in my class?

171
00:06:35,196 --> 00:06:37,443
Her sister got accepted
to East Indiana today.

172
00:06:37,590 --> 00:06:41,588
Crap. Well, let's just agree,
whatever happens,

173
00:06:41,895 --> 00:06:44,140
he is not living
in our house next year.

174
00:06:44,491 --> 00:06:45,547
I think I should call the coach

175
00:06:45,689 --> 00:06:46,720
and see what's going on.

176
00:06:46,841 --> 00:06:49,668
No, we can't do that, Mike.
We don't wanna be those parents.

177
00:06:50,897 --> 00:06:52,213
(Gasps)

178
00:06:53,605 --> 00:06:54,341
(Engine turns off)

179
00:06:54,451 --> 00:06:56,009
Afternoon, folks.
(Chuckles)

180
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:57,184
(Laughs)

181
00:06:57,288 --> 00:07:00,138
Oh, come on.
Mama needs a fat envelope.

182
00:07:00,261 --> 00:07:01,752
Beautiful day today, huh?

183
00:07:01,875 --> 00:07:04,037
Yeah, whatever. You got
anything else in there for us?

184
00:07:04,159 --> 00:07:05,932
- Nope. That's it.
- Uh, you know what?

185
00:07:06,055 --> 00:07:07,500
How about I just take
a little look myself?

186
00:07:07,607 --> 00:07:09,611
Oh, ma'am,
that's a federal offense.

187
00:07:09,727 --> 00:07:11,001
Hey, look, there's a mean dog.

188
00:07:11,113 --> 00:07:14,143
Ma'am, I have got to get
back on my route.

189
00:07:14,245 --> 00:07:16,097
There are other citizens
who are counting on me.

190
00:07:16,207 --> 00:07:19,377
(Starts engine) Oh, what are you, Batman?
Let me see in the damn bag!

191
00:07:19,745 --> 00:07:21,984
(Man shouts indistinctly)
(Tires squeak)

192
00:07:22,086 --> 00:07:25,100
This is my son's future
we're talking about!

193
00:07:25,250 --> 00:07:28,024
I know it's in there!
I know you have it!

194
00:07:28,141 --> 00:07:31,133
We never got
the Andersons' Christmas card!

195
00:07:37,668 --> 00:07:40,204
<i>Two weeks later, and still</i>
<i>nothing new with the mail.</i>

196
00:07:40,316 --> 00:07:41,881
<i>Well, not exactly nothing.</i>

197
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,163
I can't believe
they switched our mailman.

198
00:07:44,278 --> 00:07:45,509
What are they worried
about me for?

199
00:07:45,622 --> 00:07:47,524
They're the ones
that go postal.

200
00:07:50,027 --> 00:07:51,619
Not now, Sue.

201
00:07:54,114 --> 00:07:55,604
You can dust all you want,
Brick.

202
00:07:55,711 --> 00:07:57,418
You're not getting an iPad.

203
00:08:00,259 --> 00:08:03,068
<i>So while Mike and I had been</i>
<i>harassing the loyal servants</i>

204
00:08:03,181 --> 00:08:04,596
<i>of the US Postal Service,</i>

205
00:08:04,774 --> 00:08:06,669
<i>turns out</i>
<i>it wasn't the '80s anymore,</i>

206
00:08:06,794 --> 00:08:08,223
<i>and the news</i>
<i>we were all waiting for</i>

207
00:08:08,346 --> 00:08:10,648
<i>was coming over</i>
<i>a different kind of mail.</i>

208
00:08:10,805 --> 00:08:12,198
(Cell phone buzzing)

209
00:08:14,345 --> 00:08:18,069
Yes! I'm in! Oh, my God!

210
00:08:18,185 --> 00:08:20,327
I just got an official e-mail
from East Indiana State.

211
00:08:20,447 --> 00:08:22,319
It's official! I'm in!
Oh, my God.

212
00:08:22,438 --> 00:08:25,856
That's it! I'm outta here!
So long, suckers!

213
00:08:25,956 --> 00:08:28,105
East Indiana State freshmen
rule!

214
00:08:28,208 --> 00:08:29,244
(High-pitched voice)
Ha ha ha ha!

215
00:08:29,337 --> 00:08:30,658
Whoo!

216
00:08:34,576 --> 00:08:36,589
I still have to graduate,
don't I?

217
00:08:44,941 --> 00:08:45,841
No.

218
00:08:45,978 --> 00:08:47,684
But this kid in my class,
Brian Becker, got one,

219
00:08:47,789 --> 00:08:49,495
and now everybody
flocks around him.

220
00:08:49,641 --> 00:08:50,948
And he's got a restless leg,

221
00:08:51,077 --> 00:08:53,146
and he suffers
from early onset alopecia.

222
00:08:53,277 --> 00:08:56,524
I'm just saying, you guys are
always on me to make friends.

223
00:08:56,628 --> 00:08:58,030
Yeah, it's a great way
to make friends,

224
00:08:58,156 --> 00:08:59,561
but you're not getting an iPad.

225
00:08:59,672 --> 00:09:01,101
So I was talking to mom
about the iPad,

226
00:09:01,230 --> 00:09:03,825
and she thinks it'd be a great
way for me to make friends.

227
00:09:03,938 --> 00:09:05,982
<i>(Tv playing indistinctly)</i> Friends?
I thought you wanted this thing for school.

228
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:07,044
Dad liked your friend idea,

229
00:09:07,169 --> 00:09:08,960
but he thinks the iPad
should be used more for school.

230
00:09:09,078 --> 00:09:10,345
You're doing fine in school.

231
00:09:10,467 --> 00:09:12,654
Yeah, but in middle school,
the workload really picks up.

232
00:09:12,784 --> 00:09:13,947
Well,
if it's for middle school,

233
00:09:14,084 --> 00:09:15,799
we'll talk about it
when you get to middle school.

234
00:09:15,915 --> 00:09:18,067
Mom says she sees it as more
of a middle school thing.

235
00:09:18,221 --> 00:09:20,284
Yeah, well, middle school
is a long way off, isn't it?

236
00:09:20,404 --> 00:09:21,665
(Door closes) Dad's on board
with getting the iPad,

237
00:09:21,790 --> 00:09:24,088
- but he thinks middle school
is a long way off. - Really?

238
00:09:24,215 --> 00:09:26,133
- He really said that?
- Hey, mom,

239
00:09:26,265 --> 00:09:28,358
- can we go to Red Lobster
for dinner tonight? - No.

240
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:30,967
Yeah, that's what dad said.
